Actually, some people do wear an eyepatch for marksmanship training... 
As a matter of fact, eyepatches are banned from many marksmanship 
competitions because they are deemed to provide an unfair advantage.

The way in which eyepatches affect ranged attacks is twofold:

(1) they allow you to aim better, because you can concentrate on aiming 
without having to squeeze your non-master eye shut (I believe this helps 
with reducing tension).

(2) they reduce binocular vision, although this is only an issue if one 
is attempting to hit a fast-moving target or to use a weapon which 
launches in a high arc rather than straight. Certainly, experienced 
shooters will be able to estimate the range of a target without relying 
on binocular vision.

I'd abstract it as follows:

(1) eyepatches confer a + bonus to accuracy with launching weapons 
(slings, bows and crossbows), and also to ranged accuracy against 
normal-speed targets.
(2) eyepatches confer a penalty to accuracy with thrown weapons, and 
also to ranged accuracy against fast or very fast targets.

Perhaps it should provide short-range telepathy (ESP, whatever you call 
it) if you already have the intrinsic.

